<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A hand-built map for end-to-end tests against a real routing engine.

  The technique is borrowed from OSRM's own Cucumber suite, which builds a tiny
  synthetic .osm per scenario rather than routing on real extracts. It closes a
  gap the recorded fixtures in parity/fixtures/ structurally cannot: those key on
  the outgoing URL and replay whatever was recorded, so a gateway that builds a
  well formed but semantically wrong request (transposed lat/lon, swapped
  sources and destinations) gets a plausible answer and the test still passes.

  Geometry, chosen so distances are checkable by hand. At the equator 0.001 deg
  of longitude is about 111 m, and latitude likewise.

      n1 ========= n2 ========= n3     west to east, about 1113 m per leg
    (0, 0)     (0, 0.01)    (0, 0.02)
                                 |
                                 |     about 1106 m north
                                n4
                             (0.01, 0.02)

  So from n1: n2 is one leg away, n3 two, n4 three. Distances increase with each
  hop, which is what the tests assert. That holds only if the gateway sends
  coordinates in the order and orientation OSRM expects.

  Separately, and connected to nothing:

      n5 ========= n6     an island, about 5.5 km north-east of the rest
    (0.05, 0.05)  (0.05, 0.06)

  No way joins the island to East Road, so no route exists between the two
  components and OSRM reports those pairs as null in /table. That is what makes
  MTX-5 testable: an unreachable pair must survive into the model as an explicit
  sentinel, never as a large finite number a solver can optimise into a plan.

  Island Road is one-way eastbound, which makes n5 -> n6 routable and n6 -> n5
  not. That gives MTX-2 a subject: a matrix that quietly symmetrises would show
  the two as equal. The island carries it rather than the mainland so the
  distance assertions above stay undisturbed.

  All ways are highway=primary with no access restrictions, so the stock car
  profile routes them without special handling.
-->
<osm version="0.6" generator="handwritten for tests">
  <bounds minlat="-0.005" minlon="-0.005" maxlat="0.055" maxlon="0.065"/>

  <node id="1" lat="0.0"  lon="0.0"  version="1"/>
  <node id="2" lat="0.0"  lon="0.01" version="1"/>
  <node id="3" lat="0.0"  lon="0.02" version="1"/>
  <node id="4" lat="0.01" lon="0.02" version="1"/>

  <!-- The island. Deliberately joined to nothing. -->
  <node id="5" lat="0.05" lon="0.05" version="1"/>
  <node id="6" lat="0.05" lon="0.06" version="1"/>

  <way id="100" version="1">
    <nd ref="1"/>
    <nd ref="2"/>
    <nd ref="3"/>
    <tag k="highway" v="primary"/>
    <tag k="name" v="East Road"/>
  </way>

  <way id="101" version="1">
    <nd ref="3"/>
    <nd ref="4"/>
    <tag k="highway" v="primary"/>
    <tag k="name" v="North Road"/>
  </way>

  <way id="102" version="1">
    <nd ref="5"/>
    <nd ref="6"/>
    <tag k="highway" v="primary"/>
    <tag k="oneway" v="yes"/>
    <tag k="name" v="Island Road"/>
  </way>
</osm>
